 var ADD=[
  ['r3',{w:'scatterLab',par:{goal:'max'},p:'<b>Collision lab.</b> Fire a neutron at a nucleus and watch the momentum arrows. Find the hit that takes <b>the most energy</b> from the neutron.',e:'Head-on with hydrogen, the neutron stops dead. A proton has the same mass, so it takes all the momentum and all the energy. That\'s why water is such a good moderator, and why uranium-238 is hopeless at it.'}],
  ['tf3',{w:'scatterLab',par:{goal:'max'},p:'<b>Collision lab.</b> Fire a neutron at a nucleus and watch the arrows: they show the push (momentum) each one carries. Find the hit that slows the neutron down the most.',e:'Head-on with hydrogen, the neutron stops dead and hands everything to the proton, like snooker balls. Big nuclei just bounce it back at almost full speed.'}],
  ['t2-2-1',{w:'scatterLab',par:{},p:'<b>Collision lab.</b> Try each target and different aims. Then press <b>Fire 100 at random</b> to see how much energy a neutron keeps at each bounce angle.'}],
  ['r1',{w:'reactorSim',par:{goal:'critical'},p:'<b>Live core.</b> Every dot is a neutron: red ones are fast, blue ones are slow. Fast neutrons slow down by hitting hydrogen, then split uranium in the fuel. Move the control rods until the reactor is <b>critical</b>, with k holding near 1.',e:'At k = 1 each generation of neutrons is the same size as the last, so the power stays steady. That\'s what the operators hold the reactor at all day.'}],
  ['tf2',{w:'reactorSim',par:{goal:'critical'},p:'<b>Live core.</b> Every dot is a neutron: red ones are fast, blue ones are slow. When a slow one hits a fuel rod it can split a uranium atom and release 2 or 3 new ones. Move the control rods until the chain reaction is <b>steady</b>.',e:'Steady means each split leads to exactly one more split. Rods further out and it grows; further in and it dies away.'}],
  ['tk1',{w:'reactorSim',par:{goal:'critical',rods:.9},p:'<b>Live core.</b> The rods start almost fully in, so the chain reaction is dying. Pull them out slowly until the reactor is <b>critical</b>, and hold it there.',e:'Control rods soak up neutrons. Pulling them out leaves more neutrons to cause fission, so k rises. Operators move them a little at a time and wait, just like you did.'}],
  ['r6',{w:'reactorSim',par:{goal:'critical',rods:.35},p:'<b>Live core.</b> This time the rods start too far out and the power is climbing. Push them in until k settles at <b>1</b>.',e:'Rod worth is how much reactivity a bit of rod movement changes. You felt it: small moves near the middle of the core make a big difference.'}],
  ['t4-2-1',{w:'reactorSim',par:{rods:.45},p:'<b>Live core.</b> Watch the energy spectrum in the corner. Neutrons are born fast (right), lose energy hitting hydrogen and pile up at thermal energies (left), where uranium-235 catches them. Turn on slow motion to follow one.'}],
  ['a6',{w:'flux3d',par:{},p:'<b>3D flux.</b> This is the neutron flux across a square core, in 3D. It\'s highest in the middle and falls towards the edges, where neutrons leak out. Drag it to turn it.'}],
  ['t2-3-3',{w:'flux3d',par:{},p:'<b>3D flux.</b> Neutrons near the edge of a bare core leak out, so the flux falls to almost zero there. Add a reflector and watch the edges come up.'}],
  ['t3-3-1',{w:'flux3d',par:{goal:'flat'},p:'<b>3D flux.</b> This is the power across the core. The higher the peak compared with the average, the hotter the hottest fuel rod. Use the reflector and the rods in the middle to get the <b>peaking factor below 1.75</b>.',e:'A flatter shape means no single fuel rod runs too hot, so the whole core can make more power within its limits. Real cores do it with reflectors, careful fuel loading and rod patterns.'}],
  ['m4-3-2',{w:'flux3d',par:{},p:'<b>3D flux.</b> For a bare square core the diffusion equation gives φ = A cos(πx/a) cos(πy/a): a cosine hill in each direction. Drag to turn it, then see how a reflector and central rods change the solution.'}],
  ['m4-3-3',{w:'flux3d',par:{refl:.3},p:'<b>3D flux.</b> A reflector bounces leaking neutrons back, which works like making the core bigger: the cosine stretches and the edges no longer drop to zero. Try it.'}]];
