 ADD=ADD.concat([["m1",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"length","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> Zoom through the powers of ten, from a uranium nucleus to the Sun. Each step on the ruler is ten times bigger than the last."}],["m2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"growth","tau":20,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Exponential growth: the power doubles every fixed time. Change the period τ and watch how the doubling time changes with it."}],["m3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":5,"log":true,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> On a log scale, exponential decay becomes a straight line. Switch the log scale off and on. This is why reactor power meters use log scales."}],["m4",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"quad","goal":"zero","title":"rates of change"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The yellow tangent shows the rate of change at each point. Drag along the curve and find the point where the rate of change is zero."}],["m5",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"ode","ode":"decay","k":0.4,"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10],"eq":"dN/dt = −λN","title":"differential equations"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Each little line is the slope dN/dt = −λN at that point. Tap the field to drop particles: every one follows an exponential decay curve."}],["m6",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"count","rate":12,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Count a steady source second by second. The counts jump about, but the histogram settles into a shape with a spread of about √(average)."}],["e1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Follow the energy through Hinkley Point C. About a third of the heat becomes electricity; the rest warms the sea. Change the power and watch the flows."}],["e2",{"w":"scatterLab","par":{},"p":"<b>Collision lab.<\/b> Momentum is conserved in every collision. Watch the arrows: the neutron's momentum before always equals the two momenta after, added as arrows."}],["e3",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"emc2","n":3,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> A helium nucleus weighs less than the protons and neutrons it's made of. The missing mass became energy: E = mc²."}],["e3b",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"emc2","n":8,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Put numbers into E = mc². Choose a nucleus and see the missing mass turned into joules and MeV."}],["e3c",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"energy","z":-11,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> Energies from one electronvolt to a whole power station. Find one fission on the scale: tiny in joules, huge for one atom."}],["e3d",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"fission","goal":"fission"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Fire neutrons at uranium-235 and watch it split. Each fission releases about 200 MeV. Fire at least 3 and see where the fragments land."}],["a1",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"atom","n":5,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> A 3D nucleus of protons (red) and neutrons (grey), with electrons around it. Try different nuclei, all the way up to uranium."}],["a2",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"binding","n":8,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Binding energy per nucleon peaks at iron. Pick nuclei either side and see why fusing light ones or splitting heavy ones releases energy."}],["a3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":6,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> 400 nuclei, each decaying at random. Nobody can say which one goes next, but together they follow a smooth exponential curve."}],["a4",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":4,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> After one half-life, half are left; after two, a quarter. Change the half-life and check the dotted markers."}],["a5",{"w":"scatterLab","par":{},"p":"<b>Collision lab.<\/b> Scattering is the most common way a neutron interacts. Compare light and heavy targets and see how much energy each hit takes."}],["a7",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"fission","goal":"fission"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Watch U-235 absorb a neutron, wobble and split into two fragments and 2 or 3 new neutrons. Fire at least 3 times."}],["r2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"fates":true,"rods":0.5},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Watch where the neutrons end up: causing fission, caught by U-238, soaked up, or leaking out. Those fates are the six factors in action."}],["r4",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"rho":true,"goal":"critical","rods":0.75},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> The reactivity readout shows ρ = (k − 1)/k in pcm. Bring the reactor to critical, where ρ is close to zero."}],["r5",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"source":280,"rods":0.75,"n":20},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> A strong neutron source with k below 1. The count settles at source × 1/(1 − k). Pull the rods out a little at a time and watch it climb."}],["r7",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"control":"boron","boron":1500,"goal":"critical","rho":true},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> This time there are no rods: boron in the water soaks up neutrons. Dilute the boron until the reactor is critical."}],["r8",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"feedback":true,"goal":"critical","rods":0.7},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> This core has Doppler feedback: hotter fuel catches more neutrons. Pull the rods out and watch the reactor steady itself."}],["r9",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"xenon","goal":"shut"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Xenon-135 builds up, burns away and decays. Shut the reactor down and watch xenon peak about 10 hours later."}],["r10",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"xenon","goal":"shut"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Xenon peaks after shutdown, then decays away. Samarium-149 builds up in the same way but never decays, so it only grows. Watch the xenon here."}],["r11",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"trip":true,"rods":0.45,"rho":true},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Get the reactor running, then press Trip. The rods drop and k falls far below 1. How far below critical it ends up is the shutdown margin."}],["k1",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"delayed"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> After fission, some fission products decay seconds later and give out a neutron. These delayed neutrons arrive late, and that's what makes a reactor controllable."}],["k2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"sur","target":1},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Add a little reactivity and power rises on a steady period. Hold the startup rate at 1 DPM (power ×10 a minute) until the bar fills."}],["k3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"burst","goal":"prompt"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Below 1 dollar, power rises slowly. Above 1 dollar, prompt neutrons alone drive it up in milliseconds. Push reactivity past 650 pcm and see."}],["k4",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"trip","goal":"trip"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Trip the reactor from full power. Power drops promptly to a few percent, then falls on a −80 second period. Decay heat stays behind."}],["k5",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"decayheat","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> After a trip, fission products keep making heat: about 6% at first, about 1% after an hour and still around 0.5% after a day. Change how long the reactor ran beforehand."}],["t1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"sat","P":1,"T0":20,"goal":"boil"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Heat water in a sealed vessel. The higher the pressure, the hotter it must get before it boils. Get it boiling."}],["t2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"sat","P":155,"T0":290,"goal":"boil"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> At 155 bar, water doesn't boil until about 345 °C. Lower the pressure and watch the water flash to steam: that's saturation."}],["t3",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Energy in equals energy out: reactor heat = electricity + heat to the sea. Change the power and watch every flow scale with it."}],["t4",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"rankine","goal":"eff"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The Rankine cycle on a temperature–entropy chart. Make the steam hotter and the sea colder to raise the efficiency."}],["h1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pin","q":20,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Heat flows out of the fuel by conduction, then into the water by convection. Raise the heat rate and watch the centre of the pellet get hotter."}],["h2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"channel","goal":"dnb"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Bubbles carry heat away well until DNB, when a steam blanket forms and the cladding overheats. Raise the power or cut the flow until it happens."}],["h3",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"pipe","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> Squeeze the flow through a narrow section: it speeds up and the pressure drops. Push it too far and the water boils at low pressure. That's cavitation."}],["h4",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"natcirc","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> With no pumps, hot water rises from the core and cold water sinks back. Change the heat and the height and see how much flow you get."}],["p1",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"dose","z":-3,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> A dose ladder from a banana to radiation sickness. Find the UK average dose and the legal limit for workers."}],["p2",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"tds","goal":"budget","budget":50,"need":30,"mat":3,"th":0,"d":1.5,"job":30},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> A 30-minute job near a gamma source, with a 50 µSv budget. Use time, distance and shielding to get the job dose under budget."}],["p3",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"types","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> Alpha is stopped by paper, beta by aluminium, gamma needs lead. That's why alpha contamination only matters if it gets inside you. Try each shield."}],["s1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"plant","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> A whole PWR, live: the primary circuit, steam generator, turbine, generator and sea water. Change the reactor power and follow it all the way round."}],["s2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pzr","goal":"hold"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> The pressuriser holds pressure using heaters and spray. Make an insurge or outsurge, then bring pressure back to 155 bar and hold it."}],["s3",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"grid","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The generator spins at 3,000 rpm to make 50 Hz. Change the demand on the grid and watch the frequency try to move."}],["s4",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"ni"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> During a startup, power climbs through ten decades. The source, intermediate and power range detectors overlap so there's always one on scale."}],["s5",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"redundancy","n":2,"goal":"ccf"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> More safety trains means a lower chance of all failing together, until a common cause can hit them all at once. Use 3 or more trains and turn up common cause."}],["s6",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"chem","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> As boron falls through the cycle, lithium is adjusted to keep the pH in its band. Move the boron and keep the dot inside the green band."}],["o1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"star"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> A self-check drill: the procedure says to start one pump, and there are eight switches that look alike. Use STAR: Stop, Think, Act, Review."}],["o2",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"watch","event":"loca"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Three Mile Island began as a small loss of coolant. Start the event and watch pressure, levels and alarms change, with a running commentary."}],["o3",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"startup"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Take the reactor from hot shutdown to the grid. Do every step in the right order."}],["tb1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"grid","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> A spinning generator makes electricity: three waves, 50 times a second. Change the demand and watch the frequency meter."}],["tb2",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"plant","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The whole station in one live picture: heat from the reactor, steam to the turbine, electricity to the grid, and the sea to cool it."}],["tb3",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Each Hinkley Point C reactor makes about 4,590 MW of heat and 1,630 MW of electricity. Follow where the rest of the energy goes."}],["ta1",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"atom","n":5,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Turn the atom around: protons and neutrons in the middle, electrons whizzing round outside. Try different elements."}],["ta2",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"isotope","iso":"U","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Same element, different numbers of neutrons. Slide the neutrons and find uranium-235 and uranium-238."}],["ta3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":5,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Radioactive atoms decay completely at random, but a big group always halves in the same time. Change the half-life and watch."}],["tf1",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"fission","goal":"fission"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Fire a neutron at uranium-235 and watch it split into two smaller atoms, with 2 or 3 new neutrons and lots of energy. Fire at least 3."}],["tc1",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"pellet","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> Zoom out from a single fuel pellet to a fuel rod, a fuel assembly and the whole core."}],["tc2",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"vessel"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> Follow the water through the reactor vessel: in cold, down the outside, up through the core and out hot."}],["tc3",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"refuel","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> At each refuelling about a third of the fuel is swapped and the rest is moved around. Press Next refuelling a few times and watch the colours shuffle."}],["tk2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"control":"boron","boron":1500,"goal":"critical"},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> No rods this time: boron dissolved in the water soaks up neutrons. Lower the boron until the chain reaction is steady."}],["tk3",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"feedback":true,"goal":"critical","rods":0.7},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Hotter fuel catches more neutrons, so the reactor steadies itself. Pull the rods out and watch it settle on its own."}],["tp1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"sat","P":155,"T0":300,"goal":"boil"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> High pressure stops the water boiling, even at 300 °C. Lower the pressure and see what happens."}],["tp2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pzr","goal":"hold"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> The pressuriser's heaters push pressure up and its spray brings it down. Disturb it, then hold 155 bar."}],["tp3",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"natcirc","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> The main pumps push water round the loop. Turn them off and see natural circulation keep a little water moving on its own."}],["ts1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"plant","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Follow the secondary circuit: steam from the steam generator turns the turbine, then the sea turns it back into water."}],["ts2",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"grid","goal":"loop"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The generator is connected to the grid. Lose the grid and watch the diesels start up to keep the plant safe."}],["ts3",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Almost two thirds of the heat goes into the sea. Watch the flows as you change the power."}],["td1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"watch","event":"turbine"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Control, cool, contain. Start a turbine trip and watch the reactor trip (control) and the heat being removed (cool)."}],["td2",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"tree","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> Each safety system is another barrier. Watch events flow through the tree: it takes several failures in a row to reach core damage."}],["td3",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"redundancy","n":4,"goal":"ccf"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> The EPR has four safety trains, and one is enough. See how the failure chance drops, and what common cause does to it."}],["tr1",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"dose","z":-3,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> Zoom through doses from a banana to a CT scan to radiation sickness. Where does a year in the UK sit?"}],["tr2",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"tds","goal":"budget","budget":50,"need":30,"mat":3,"th":0,"d":1.5,"job":30},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> Do a 30-minute job without going over 50 µSv. Move further away, add shielding or work faster."}],["tr3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":10,"log":true,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Waste becomes less radioactive with time, very quickly at first and then slowly. Watch the curve on the log scale."}],["to1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"crew"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Meet the crew. Tap each person to see what they do and who they talk to."}],["to2",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"startup"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> From cold to full power, one step at a time. Do the startup in the right order."}],["to3",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"diagnose"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Something is about to go wrong. Press Start, read the trends and alarms, then say what happened."}],["pd1",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"lineup","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> A simple piping diagram, live. Open and shut the valves to send water from the tank to the steam generator, not down the drain."}],["pd2",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"signal","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> An instrument like PT-101 sends its reading as a 4–20 mA current. Change the pressure, then break the wire and see what the display does."}],["pd3",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"lineup","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> Line up the system: tap the valves to open or shut them, start the pump and get flow to the steam generator only."}],["ic1",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"signal","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> Sensors talk to the control room with 4–20 mA. 4 mA means zero and 20 mA means full scale, so a broken wire shows up straight away."}],["ic2",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"vote","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> Four pressure channels, and the trip needs 2 of 4. Fail one channel and check one bad reading can't trip the reactor on its own."}],["ic3",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"level","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> An automatic controller holds the tank level. Open the drain, then tune the gain and integral until the level comes back to the setpoint."}],["ee1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"grid","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Three-phase electricity at 50 Hz goes out to the grid. Change the demand and watch the frequency."}],["ee2",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"grid","goal":"loop"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The plant needs power for its own pumps. Lose the grid and watch the diesels take over the safety loads."}],["ee3",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"watch","event":"loop"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Loss of offsite power: the pumps coast down, the reactor trips and the diesels start. Watch it on the trends."}],["hp1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"star"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Eight switches that look alike, with time pressure: perfect conditions for a slip. Use STAR to start the right pump."}],["hp2",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"star"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Self-checking: touch the switch, read the label, compare it with the procedure, then act. Try it."}],["hp3",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"comms"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Three-way communication: the supervisor gives an order, you repeat it back exactly and they confirm. Pick the right repeat-back each time."}],["m2-1-1",{"w":"machineLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch","eq":{"a":3,"b":4,"r":19}},"p":"<b>Maths machine.<\/b> An equation is a balance. Do the same thing to both sides to get x on its own."}],["m2-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"lin","goal":"touch","title":"straight lines"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> y = mx + c. Change the gradient m and the intercept c and watch the line move. The tangent is the line itself."}],["m2-1-3",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"invsq","goal":"touch","mat":3,"th":0},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> Dose rate follows the inverse square law: double the distance and it drops to a quarter. Move the detector and check."}],["m2-2-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"ex","goal":"touch","title":"the number e"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> y = eˣ is special: at every point its gradient equals its height. Drag along it and compare y with the gradient."}],["m2-2-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"ln","goal":"touch","title":"natural log"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> ln x undoes eˣ. Its gradient is 1/x. Drag along and check the gradient at x = 1, 2 and 4."}],["m2-2-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"loglin","goal":"ln","title":"straight lines from curves"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Exponential data makes a curve. Take ln of y and it becomes a straight line whose gradient is the rate k."}],["m2-3-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"secant","f":"sq","x0":1,"goal":"h","title":"how steep is a curve"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> A chord between two points on y = x². Shrink the gap h and watch the chord's gradient close in on the tangent's: 2."}],["m2-3-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"pow","goal":"touch","title":"differentiating"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The top graph is xⁿ, the bottom graph its gradient, nxⁿ⁻¹. Change n and watch the rule work."}],["m2-3-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"period","dy":[0,2.5],"goal":"touch","title":"rates in reactors"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Power on a period: P = e^(t/τ). Its rate of change, P/τ, grows with the power itself. Change τ."}],["m2-4-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"power","lo":0,"unit":"MWh","goal":"touch","Ay":[0,9500],"title":"area under a graph"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The area under a power–time graph is energy. Watch the shaded area and the running total grow together."}],["m2-4-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"sq","lo":0,"x":[0,3],"y":[0,9],"Ay":[0,9.5],"goal":"touch","title":"integrating"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The running total of the area under x² is x³/3. Integration undoes differentiation."}],["m2-4-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"riemann","f":"power","lo":0,"hi":12,"n":4,"goal":"riemann","title":"totals in reactors"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Add up the energy in strips. Use more strips and watch the total close in on the exact answer."}],["m2-5-1",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":8,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Activity is decays per second, and it falls with the same half-life as the number of nuclei. Compare the two readouts."}],["m2-5-2",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"length","z":-10,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> Atoms are around 10⁻¹⁰ m across, so a fuel pellet holds about 10²² of them. Zoom between an atom and a pellet."}],["m2-5-3",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"energy","z":7,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> 1 kg of coal against 1 kg of U-235. Zoom between them: a difference of about three million times."}],["t2-1-1",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"stability","n":8,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Stable nuclei sit in a valley on the chart of neutrons against protons. Pick nuclei off the valley and see how they decay back towards it."}],["t2-1-2",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"types","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Alpha, beta-minus, beta-plus and gamma. Pick each type and see what it throws out and where the nucleus moves on the chart."}],["t2-1-3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"chain","T":6,"T2":3,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> A parent decays to a daughter that decays too. Change the two half-lives and watch the daughter build up, then fade."}],["t2-2-2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"rods":0.45},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Cross-section is the target size a nucleus offers. Uranium-235 offers slow neutrons (blue) a huge target, so they cause most of the fissions. Watch where the flashes happen."}],["t2-2-3",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"fastBtn":true,"rods":0.45},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Take the water out: without a moderator the neutrons stay fast, the uranium rarely catches them and the chain reaction dies."}],["t2-3-1",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"fates":true,"rods":0.5},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Follow a generation: born fast, slowed by the water, then either causing fission, caught by U-238, soaked up or leaking out."}],["t2-3-2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"fates":true,"rods":0.6,"goal":"critical"},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> The fates panel shows the six factors as percentages. Move the rods until the reactor is critical and see how the fates balance."}],["t2-4-1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"heat","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Heating water to boiling takes energy, and boiling it away takes about five times as much again. Watch the temperature stop rising while it boils."}],["t2-4-2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"sat","P":70,"T0":200,"goal":"boil"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Steam tables are this curve: each pressure has its own boiling temperature. Get the water boiling at a few different pressures."}],["t2-4-3",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The first law: energy in = energy out. The reactor's heat is always exactly the electricity plus the heat to the sea."}],["t2-5-1",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"damage","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> Fast neutrons knock atoms out of place, leaving vacancies. Turn on annealing and watch some of the damage heal."}],["t2-5-2",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"corrosion","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> Corrosion grows an oxide layer, and loose corrosion products become crud on the fuel. Change the pH and compare."}],["t2-5-3",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"chem","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> Boron and lithium are balanced so the pH stays between 7.1 and 7.4. Keep the dot in the band as the boron falls."}],["m3-1-1",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"rho":true,"goal":"critical","rods":0.78},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Watch k and ρ = (k − 1)/k together. Bring the reactor to critical, where k is 1 and ρ is 0."}],["m3-1-2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"jump","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Reactivity in pcm and in dollars side by side: 1 dollar = β ≈ 650 pcm. Add some and watch the power respond."}],["m3-1-3",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"feedback":true,"rho":true,"goal":"critical","rods":0.7},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> A reactivity balance in action: rod reactivity going in is cancelled by Doppler going out. Pull the rods and watch the balance settle."}],["m3-2-1",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"rho":true,"rods":0.9},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Rod worth: move the rods in small steps and watch how much k changes each time. Moves near the middle of the core count the most."}],["m3-2-2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"control":"boron","boron":1600,"rho":true,"goal":"critical"},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Boron worth is pcm per ppm. Dilute from 1,600 ppm and see how much the reactivity changes for each step."}],["m3-2-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"ode","ode":"approach","k":0.35,"yinf":2,"x":[0,12],"y":[0,10],"eq":"dC/dt = k(C_feed − C)","title":"dilution"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Feed and bleed: the boron concentration heads exponentially towards the feed concentration. Drop particles at different starting values."}],["m3-3-1",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"sur","target":0.5},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Period comes from reactivity. Add enough to get a startup rate of 0.5 DPM and hold it until the bar fills."}],["m3-3-2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"sur","target":1},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> SUR = 26/τ decades per minute. Find the reactivity that gives 1 DPM and hold it."}],["m3-3-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"jump","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Step reactivity in and power jumps at once (the prompt jump), then rises on a period. Step it out and watch the prompt drop."}],["m3-4-1",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"source":280,"rods":0.8,"n":20},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Subcritical multiplication: the source neutrons are multiplied by 1/(1 − k). Pull the rods out in steps and watch the multiplication grow."}],["m3-4-2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"source":280,"rods":0.85,"n":20},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> The count rate rises as you approach critical. 1/M = 1 − k falls towards zero, which is what a 1/M plot predicts. Withdraw the rods in steps."}],["m3-4-3",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"source":280,"rods":0.8,"rho":true,"goal":"critical"},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Approach criticality in careful steps, then hold it critical. Compare the multiplication readout as you get close."}],["m3-5-1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"balance","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The primary heat balance: reactor power = flow × specific heat × temperature rise. Change the power and watch the flows."}],["m3-5-2",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"plant","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The secondary calorimetric measures reactor power from the steam side. Follow the feed and steam flows round the plant."}],["m3-5-3",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"rankine","goal":"eff"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> Efficiency is limited by the temperatures. Raise the steam temperature and cool the condenser to push it up."}],["t3-1-1",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"feedback":true,"goal":"critical","rods":0.7},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> A negative temperature coefficient makes a reactor self-regulating. Pull the rods out and watch the core heat up and settle on its own."}],["t3-1-2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"feedback","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Doppler feedback at power. Add reactivity: power rises, the fuel heats up and power settles at a new, slightly higher level."}],["t3-1-3",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"fastBtn":true,"rods":0.45},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Steam voids mean less water to slow neutrons down. Take the water out and see the chain reaction die. That's why a PWR's void coefficient is negative."}],["t3-2-1",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"xenon","goal":"shut"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> One second here is one hour in the plant. Cut power to 50%, then shut down, and watch xenon rise and fall."}],["t3-2-2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"xenon","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Changing power makes xenon swing, and big cores can swing from side to side. Make some power changes and watch the xenon lag behind."}],["t3-2-3",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"loading","goal":"checker"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> Fresh fuel is the most reactive, so it's spread out and burnable poisons hold it down. Compare the loading patterns."}],["t3-3-2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"channel","goal":"dnb"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> DNBR is the heat flux needed for DNB divided by the actual heat flux. Raise the power or cut the flow until it drops below 1."}],["t3-3-3",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pin","q":20,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Technical specifications keep the linear heat rate well below what would melt the fuel. See how much margin there is."}],["t3-4-1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"watch","event":"feed"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> A heat-up event: main feedwater is lost. Start it and watch the SG levels fall until the reactor trips and auxiliary feed starts."}],["t3-4-2",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"watch","event":"slb"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> A steam line break overcools the plant. Start it and watch temperature and pressure fall, the trip, and safety injection."}],["t3-4-3",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"diagnose"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Is it a LOCA, a tube rupture or something else? Start the event, read the trends and alarms, and diagnose it."}],["t3-5-1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"csf"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> The six critical safety functions, shown as status colours. Tap the one the crew should deal with first."}],["t3-5-2",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"diagnose"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Event-based procedures need a diagnosis first. Watch the event and work out which one it is."}],["t3-5-3",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"star"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Under pressure, slow down: Stop, Think, Act, Review. Start the right pump."}],["m4-1-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"ode","ode":"decay","k":0.4,"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10],"eq":"dy/dx = −k·y","title":"differential equations"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> A differential equation gives the slope at every point. Tap anywhere to start a solution and watch it follow the slopes."}],["m4-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"ode","ode":"approach","k":0.4,"yinf":6,"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10],"eq":"dy/dt = k(6 − y)","title":"equilibrium"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Every solution heads to the same equilibrium, y = 6, from above or below. Drop particles and change k."}],["m4-1-3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"chain","T":8,"T2":3,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> The parent and daughter equations solved live: the daughter rises, peaks and falls. Change the half-lives."}],["m4-2-1",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"atten","goal":"touch","mat":3,"th":2},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> Σ = Nσ: more nuclei per cm³, or bigger targets, means more interactions per cm. Compare the materials."}],["m4-2-2",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"atten","goal":"touch","mat":3,"th":2},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> I = I₀e^(−Σx). Change the thickness and the material, then read off the mean free path and the half-value thickness."}],["m4-2-3",{"w":"flux3d","par":{},"p":"<b>3D flux.<\/b> Reaction rate = Σφ, so power density follows the flux. Turn the surface and see where most of the power comes from."}],["m4-3-1",{"w":"flux3d","par":{},"p":"<b>3D flux.<\/b> Fick's law: neutrons diffuse from high flux to low flux, so they drift out of the middle towards the edges. Add a reflector and see the edges fill in."}],["m4-4-1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pin","q":15,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Fourier's law: heat flows down the temperature gradient. The steepest gradient is in the pellet, which conducts heat poorly."}],["m4-4-2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pin","q":25,"goal":"melt"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Inside the pellet the temperature is a parabola. Push the heat rate up until the centre reaches the melting point."}],["m4-4-3",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"pin","q":20,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Add up the steps from fuel centre to coolant: pellet, gap, cladding, water film. Cut the flow and see which step changes."}],["m4-5-1",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"pipe","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> Continuity: the same mass flows everywhere, so narrow pipe means fast flow. Bernoulli: fast flow means low pressure."}],["m4-5-2",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"reynolds","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> Speed the water up and watch smooth laminar flow turn turbulent as the Reynolds number passes about 2,300."}],["m4-5-3",{"w":"flowLab","par":{"mode":"pump","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Flow lab.<\/b> The pump works where its curve crosses the system curve. Change the pump speed and the valve and move the operating point."}],["t4-1-1",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"drop","goal":"split"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> A nucleus behaves like a drop of liquid. Stretch it: surface tension pulls back, but electric repulsion wins past the barrier and it splits."}],["t4-1-2",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"drop","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> Compare U-235 and U-238. Does a slow neutron bring enough energy to get over the fission barrier?"}],["t4-1-3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"delayed"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Fission products called precursors decay and give out delayed neutrons over the next minute. Watch the delayed neutron rate fall away."}],["t4-2-2",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"fastBtn":true,"rods":0.45},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> A thermal reactor needs its moderator. Take the water out and watch the spectrum shift to fast and the chain reaction die."}],["t4-2-3",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"breed":true,"rods":0.45},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Every neutron U-238 catches makes plutonium-239. Watch the plutonium count and the conversion ratio."}],["t4-3-1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"channel","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Up the hot channel the water starts to boil at the wall. Raise the power and lower the pressure to get more boiling."}],["t4-3-2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"channel","goal":"dnb"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Along the boiling curve, nucleate boiling is great until the critical heat flux. Find DNB."}],["t4-3-3",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"channel","goal":"dnb"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> DNB happens when the heat flux beats the limit somewhere up the channel. Find the conditions that cause it."}],["t4-4-1",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"tensile","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> A tensile test: the steel stretches elastically, yields, necks, then snaps. Compare vessel steel, Zircaloy and irradiated steel."}],["t4-4-2",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"fatigue","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> Fatigue: every load cycle grows a crack a tiny bit. Raise the stress amplitude and watch the life on the S–N curve drop."}],["t4-4-3",{"w":"materialsLab","par":{"mode":"dbtt","goal":"brittle","T":40},"p":"<b>Materials lab.<\/b> A Charpy test. Irradiation pushes the ductile-to-brittle transition to higher temperatures. Find conditions where the steel snaps."}],["t4-5-1",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"atten","goal":"touch","mat":3,"th":2},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> Gamma rays are removed one interaction at a time, so they fall off exponentially. Compare lead, concrete and water."}],["t4-5-2",{"w":"radLab","par":{"mode":"atten","goal":"touch","mat":5,"th":8,"buildup":true},"p":"<b>Radiation lab.<\/b> Buildup: scattered gammas add to the dose, so the real dose is above the simple exponential. Turn buildup on and off."}],["t4-5-3",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"dose","z":-2,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> Dose limits on the ladder: 20 mSv a year for workers, 1 mSv for the public. Compare them with everyday doses."}],["m5-1-1",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> This is the point kinetics equations solved live with six delayed neutron groups. Add reactivity and watch the period settle."}],["m5-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"euler","ode":"growth","x":[0,4],"y":[0,8],"eq":"dy/dx = 0.5y","goal":"touch","title":"Euler's method"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Euler's method steps along the slope. Big steps drift away from the exact curve; small steps track it. Change h."}],["m5-1-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"sur","target":0.5},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> The inhour equation links reactivity to period. Find the reactivity that gives a steady 0.5 DPM."}],["m5-2-1",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"feedback","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Kinetics with feedback: fuel temperature takes reactivity away as power rises, so power settles instead of running away."}],["m5-2-2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"burst","goal":"prompt"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> A prompt-critical burst, slowed 20 times. Above 1 dollar power shoots up until the Doppler effect shuts it down."}],["m5-2-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"burst","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> A rod ejection is a big, fast reactivity step. Try different sizes and see how the burst changes."}],["m5-3-1",{"w":"reactorSim","par":{"rods":0.45},"p":"<b>Live core.<\/b> Two groups: fast neutrons (red) and thermal neutrons (blue). The spectrum in the corner shows the two humps. Turn on slow motion."}],["m5-3-2",{"w":"flux3d","par":{"refl":0.5},"p":"<b>3D flux.<\/b> A reflector sends leaking neutrons back, which raises the flux at the edge and flattens the shape. Compare no reflector with a thick one."}],["m5-3-3",{"w":"flux3d","par":{},"p":"<b>3D flux.<\/b> A computer solves diffusion on a grid like this mesh. Each square is one cell of the solution."}],["m5-4-1",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"failure","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> 100 pumps failing at random with rate λ. Without repairs they follow e^(−λt). Add repairs and read the unavailability."}],["m5-4-2",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"redundancy","n":2,"goal":"ccf"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> Independent trains multiply: p, p², p³. Common cause breaks that. Use 3 or more trains, turn up β and compare."}],["m5-4-3",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"tree","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> An event tree, live: initiating events pass each safety function or fail it. Change the success chances and watch the core damage chance."}],["m5-5-1",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"decayheat","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Decay heat in detail: 6.6% × (t^−0.2 − (t + T)^−0.2). Change the operating time T and read the heat at each time."}],["m5-5-2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"heat","big":true,"m":100,"pw":20,"T0":60,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Decay heat into a pool of water: how long until it boils, and until it boils dry? Change the water and the decay heat."}],["m5-5-3",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"adiabatic","tl":60,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> With no cooling at all, decay heat warms the fuel with nowhere to go. See how long it takes to reach the limits."}],["t5-1-1",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"refuel","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> Batches and burnup: each assembly stays about three cycles. Run a few refuellings and watch each batch move through the core."}],["t5-1-2",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"loading","goal":"checker"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> Where the new fuel goes sets the power shape and the leakage. Try each loading pattern and find the low-leakage one."}],["t5-1-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"ni"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Startup physics tests begin with the instruments. Watch power climb through the source, intermediate and power ranges."}],["t5-2-1",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"diagnose"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Design basis faults are the events the plant is designed to cope with. Watch one happen and diagnose it."}],["t5-2-2",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"adiabatic","tl":30,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> Safety analysis assumes the worst: no cooling straight after a trip. Check how much time that leaves."}],["t5-2-3",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"risk","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> Risk is unacceptable above 1 in 10,000 a year, broadly acceptable below 1 in a million, and must be ALARP in between. Move the risk."}],["t5-3-1",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"tree","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> A Level 1 PSA asks how often the core gets damaged. Watch initiating events pass through the safety functions."}],["t5-3-2",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"tree","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> Inside a Level 1 PSA: multiply the chances along each branch to get each end state. Change the reliabilities."}],["t5-3-3",{"w":"statsLab","par":{"mode":"redundancy","n":2,"goal":"ccf"},"p":"<b>Risk lab.<\/b> Using risk to decide: does adding a train help? Not much once common cause dominates. Try it."}],["t5-4-1",{"w":"thermoLab","par":{"mode":"adiabatic","tl":60,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Heat lab.<\/b> How a core melts: without cooling, decay heat raises the fuel past the cladding limit and then to melting. Change when cooling is lost."}],["t5-4-2",{"w":"panelLab","par":{"mode":"watch","event":"loca"},"p":"<b>Control room.<\/b> Steam from a break raises containment pressure. Start a loss of coolant and watch the containment trend."}],["t5-4-3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":8,"log":true,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> The source term changes with time: short-lived isotopes such as iodine-131 fade first. Watch decay on the log scale."}],["t5-5-1",{"w":"cycleLab","par":{"mode":"fuel","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Plant lab.<\/b> The fuel cycle from mine to store. Turn reprocessing on and see the uranium and plutonium loop back."}],["t5-5-2",{"w":"core3d","par":{"mode":"core"},"p":"<b>3D core.<\/b> A large PWR core has 193 assemblies; an SMR uses far fewer. Turn the core and see how the power peaks in the middle."}],["t5-5-3",{"w":"atomLab","par":{"mode":"fusion","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Atom lab.<\/b> D + T → helium + neutron + 17.6 MeV. Heat the plasma until the nuclei hit hard enough to fuse."}],["c1-1-1",{"w":"machineLab","par":{"mode":"machine","ops":[["×",2],["+",3]],"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Maths machine.<\/b> A function is a machine: put x in, get y out. Change the input, then run it backwards to undo it."}],["c1-1-2",{"w":"machineLab","par":{"mode":"order","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Maths machine.<\/b> Order of operations, step by step: indices, then multiply, then add."}],["c1-1-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"quad","goal":"touch","title":"graphs of functions"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> A graph shows every input and output at once. Change a, b and c and watch the curve move."}],["c1-2-1",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"indices","op":"mul","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ. Count the x's: multiplying just puts them in one row."}],["c1-2-2",{"w":"scaleLab","par":{"mode":"indices","op":"div","goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Scale lab.<\/b> Dividing cancels x's. Make b equal to a for x⁰ = 1, and bigger than a for a negative power."}],["c1-2-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"pow","n":0.5,"goal":"touch","title":"fractional powers"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> x^0.5 is √x. Slide the power between whole numbers and watch the curve change shape smoothly."}],["c1-3-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"lin","goal":"touch","title":"rise over run"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Gradient = rise ÷ run. Change m and check the tracer's gradient readout."}],["c1-3-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"lin","goal":"touch","title":"y = mx + c"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> m tilts the line and c slides it up and down. Make a line through the origin with gradient 2."}],["c1-3-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"motion","goal":"touch","title":"gradient as a rate"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The gradient of a distance–time graph is the speed. Watch the ball and the three graphs move together."}],["c1-4-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"secant","f":"sq","x0":1,"title":"average rate of change"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The average rate of change between two points is the gradient of the chord between them. Change the gap h."}],["c1-4-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"motion","goal":"touch","title":"average speed"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Speed changes all the time here. Compare the slope of the distance graph with the speed graph underneath."}],["c1-4-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"secant","f":"sq","x0":1.5,"goal":"h","title":"getting closer"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The big idea: let the gap h shrink towards zero and the chord becomes the tangent. Drag h right down."}],["c2-1-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"sq","goal":"touch","title":"the tangent"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The tangent just touches the curve. Drag along y = x² and watch its gradient: always 2x."}],["c2-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"secant","f":"cube","x0":1,"goal":"h","title":"limits"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> As h → 0 the chord gradient tends to a limit: 3 for x³ at x = 1. Shrink h and watch it converge."}],["c2-1-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"secant","f":"sq","x0":2,"goal":"h","title":"x² from scratch"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> For y = x² at x = 2 the chord gradient is 4 + h. Shrink h to 0.01 and see it reach 4."}],["c2-2-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"sq","goal":"touch","title":"dy/dx"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> f(x) on top, f′(x) underneath. The bottom graph is the gradient of the top one at every x."}],["c2-2-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"secant","f":"cube","x0":1.2,"goal":"h","title":"first principles for x³"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> From first principles, (x + h)³ − x³ over h tends to 3x². Shrink h and check against 3x²."}],["c2-2-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"pow","goal":"touch","title":"spotting the pattern"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Try n = 1, 2, 3 and 4 and look at the gradient graph each time. Spot the pattern: nxⁿ⁻¹."}],["c2-3-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"pow","n":3,"goal":"touch","title":"the power rule"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The power rule, live: the gradient of xⁿ is nxⁿ⁻¹. Change n and drag the tracer."}],["c2-3-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"quad","goal":"touch","title":"sums and constants"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Differentiate term by term: ax² + bx + c gives 2ax + b. Change c and see that the gradient graph doesn't move."}],["c2-3-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"deriv","f":"pow","n":-1,"goal":"touch","title":"negative powers"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The power rule works for negative and fractional powers too. Try n = −1 and n = 0.5."}],["c2-4-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"cubic","goal":"touch","title":"gradient at a point"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Read the gradient at any point from the tracer. Check it against 3x² − a."}],["c2-4-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"quad","goal":"touch","title":"tangents and normals"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The tangent has the curve's gradient m; the normal is at right angles, with gradient −1/m. Drag the point."}],["c2-4-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"stationary","f":"cubic","goal":"touch","title":"increasing and decreasing"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Green where the gradient is positive (increasing), red where it's negative (decreasing). Bend the curve and watch."}],["c3-1-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"stationary","f":"cubic","goal":"zero","title":"stationary points"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Stationary points are where dy/dx = 0. Drag the tracer to one: the tangent goes flat."}],["c3-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"stationary","f":"cubic","goal":"touch","title":"second derivative"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The second derivative says which way the curve bends: negative at a maximum, positive at a minimum."}],["c3-1-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"stationary","f":"quad","goal":"touch","title":"maximum or minimum"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Make a negative to flip the parabola and see the minimum become a maximum."}],["c3-2-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"optim","goal":"max","title":"optimisation"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Cut squares from the corners of a 12 cm sheet and fold it into a box. Find the cut that gives the biggest volume."}],["c3-2-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"motion","goal":"touch","title":"s, v and a"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Displacement, velocity and acceleration, live. Velocity is ds/dt and acceleration is dv/dt. Watch the ball turn round when v = 0."}],["c3-2-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"sur","target":1},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> A real rate of change in the plant: the startup rate. Hold the power rising at 1 decade per minute."}],["c3-3-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"ex","goal":"touch","title":"e"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> eˣ is its own derivative: the gradient readout always equals y. Drag along and check."}],["c3-3-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"explore","f":"ln","goal":"touch","title":"ln x"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> d/dx (ln x) = 1/x. Drag the tracer and compare the gradient with 1 ÷ x."}],["c3-3-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"trig","goal":"touch","title":"sin and cos"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> A point going round a circle draws sin and cos. The height is sin θ and the across distance is cos θ."}],["c3-4-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"chain","f":"chain","goal":"touch","title":"the chain rule"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> y = sin(x²): dy/dx = dy/du × du/dx. Drag the tracer and watch both parts multiply together."}],["c3-4-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"chain","f":"prod","goal":"touch","title":"the product rule"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> y = uv, so dy/dx = u′v + uv′. The readout adds the two parts at the tracer."}],["c3-4-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"chain","f":"quot","goal":"touch","title":"the quotient rule"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> y = u/v, so dy/dx = (u′v − uv′)/v². Follow the numbers as you drag."}],["c4-1-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"lin","m":1,"c":1,"lo":0,"x":[0,6],"y":[0,8],"Ay":[0,25],"goal":"touch","title":"integration"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The running total of the area under a graph is its integral. Differentiate that total and you get back to where you started."}],["c4-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"pow","n":2,"lo":0,"x":[0,3],"y":[0,9],"Ay":[0,9.5],"goal":"touch","title":"integrating xⁿ"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The area under xⁿ from 0 is xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1). Change n and check the running total."}],["c4-1-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"inv","k":1,"lo":0.5,"x":[0.5,6],"y":[0,2.5],"Ay":[0,3],"goal":"touch","title":"1/x"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The area under k/x grows like k ln x: slower and slower, but it never stops. Start with k = 1."}],["c4-2-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"riemann","f":"sq","lo":0,"hi":3,"x":[0,3],"y":[0,9],"n":4,"goal":"riemann","title":"thin strips"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Area as thin strips: more strips means a better answer. Push the strips up to 40 or more and watch the error shrink."}],["c4-2-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"sq","lo":0,"x":[0,3],"y":[0,9],"Ay":[0,9.5],"goal":"touch","title":"definite integrals"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> A definite integral is the area between two limits. Drag the upper limit and watch the value change."}],["c4-2-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"quad","a":-1,"b":4,"c":0,"lo":0,"x":[0,4],"y":[0,5],"Ay":[0,12],"goal":"touch","lock":["a","b","c"],"title":"areas under curves"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The area under the hill y = 4x − x² from 0 to 4 is 32/3. Sweep across it and check."}],["c4-3-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"ex","lo":-2,"x":[-2,2],"y":[0,8],"Ay":[0,8],"goal":"touch","title":"eˣ"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The integral of eˣ is eˣ. Watch the running total keep pace with the curve."}],["c4-3-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"sin","lo":0,"x":[0,6.3],"y":[-1.5,1.5],"Ay":[-0.5,2.5],"goal":"touch","lock":["A","w","ph"],"title":"sin and cos"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The area under sin x is 1 − cos x: it rises to 2 at π, then the negative half cancels it back to 0."}],["c4-3-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"exp","A":1,"k":0.5,"lo":0,"x":[0,4],"y":[0,8],"Ay":[0,14],"goal":"touch","title":"a linear expression inside"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> ∫e^(kx) dx = e^(kx)/k. Change k and see the area change."}],["c4-4-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"chain","f":"chain","goal":"touch","title":"substitution"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Substitution is the chain rule backwards. Here u = x²: see how du/dx appears in the gradient."}],["c4-4-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"chain","f":"prod","goal":"touch","title":"by parts"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Integration by parts is the product rule backwards. See the two parts, u′v and uv′, add up."}],["c4-4-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"riemann","f":"prod","lo":0,"hi":10,"n":8,"method":"trap","goal":"riemann","title":"checking numerically"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Not sure of your method? Check numerically: the trapezium rule with lots of strips gets very close."}],["c5-1-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"ode","ode":"decay","k":0.4,"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10],"eq":"dy/dx = −k·y","title":"differential equations"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> A differential equation describes the slope everywhere. Drop particles and see the family of solutions."}],["c5-1-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"ode","ode":"growth","k":0.25,"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10],"eq":"dy/dx = k·y","title":"separating the variables"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Separating variables gives y = Ae^(kx): every particle follows one of these curves."}],["c5-1-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"growth","tau":15,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Growth and decay in a reactor: power on a period, P = P₀e^(t/τ). Change τ."}],["c5-2-1",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":6,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> The decay law, N = N₀e^(−λt), with 400 real random nuclei on top. Compare the white curve with the purple counts."}],["c5-2-2",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"single","T":4,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Half-life = ln 2 / λ. Change it and check the 50%, 25% and 12.5% markers."}],["c5-2-3",{"w":"decayLab","par":{"mode":"count","rate":20,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Decay lab.<\/b> Activity is the number of decays per second. Count them second by second and see how much they vary."}],["c5-3-1",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"growth","tau":30,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Power on a period. Change τ and watch the doubling markers move closer together or further apart."}],["c5-3-2",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"growth","tau":26,"goal":"touch"},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Doubling time = τ ln 2 and SUR = 26/τ. Set τ = 26 s for 1 DPM."}],["c5-3-3",{"w":"kinetics","par":{"mode":"period","goal":"sur","target":1},"p":"<b>Kinetics.<\/b> Why the period matters: too short and power gets away from you. Hold a safe 1 DPM."}],["c5-4-1",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"euler","ode":"growth","x":[0,4],"y":[0,8],"eq":"dy/dx = 0.5y","goal":"touch","title":"Euler's method"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Euler's method: take a step along the slope, then repeat. Shrink the step and see the error fall."}],["c5-4-2",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"riemann","f":"power","lo":0,"hi":12,"n":4,"method":"trap","goal":"riemann","title":"the trapezium rule"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> The trapezium rule joins the points with straight lines. Add strips until the error is tiny."}],["c5-4-3",{"w":"graphLab","par":{"mode":"area","f":"power","lo":0,"unit":"MWh","Ay":[0,9500],"goal":"touch","title":"energy from power"},"p":"<b>Graph lab.<\/b> Energy is the integral of power over time. Watch the energy total build up as power rises."}]]);
